<h1>Video 090 — Inter Thread Communication Part-1</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-10 || Inter Thread Communication Part-1</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/090-Ev0mltHfYh0.txt). ASR decoded in place: weight / weit / waight = wait; threats = threads; Postman / post man = postman; D / Durga = waiting person (T1); relig / religious / reles / relases = releases; lack / luck = lock; illegal monitor State / shate = IllegalMonitorStateException; SEMA Force = semaphores; ENT / tjp / scjp = SCJP/interview; Noy / Noyify = notify; Mond / NS = ms / ns; 18 person / waiting = waiting thread; keep subing = keep writing; vo / void = void; runable = runnable; scheder / threaded = thread scheduler; Packers and movers / PST = post office digression.</p>
<p>This part is mostly theory + signatures + life-cycle transitions for wait / notify / notifyAll. (Full producer-style program is later sessions.)</p>
<h3>00:06 — Topic intro: inter-thread communication</h3>
<p>Next topic: inter-thread communication — how two threads communicate with each other.</p>
<p>Not a “traditional” topic like “ways to define a thread” or priorities. Requires thinking (like synchronization). Many people lack full confidence here.</p>
<h3>01:40 — Letter / post-box analogy (polling is bad)</h3>
<p>Story (evening ~6:00 call): dear says she posted a letter; may arrive tomorrow morning. He keeps checking the personal post-box at the gate every 15–45 minutes all night into next afternoon (~1:45 p.m.) until the letter arrives. Content: “leaving for native place — don’t call/message until back.” Huge anxiety wasted on something that could have been said on the phone.</p>
<p>Mapping:</p>
<ul><li>He expects updation of post-box</li></ul>
<ul><li>Postman performs the update</li></ul>
<ul><li>Polling every few minutes = waste of system time → not recommended</li></ul>
<p>Better approach: put a sticker — “eagerly waiting; if you update, please notify me” → sleep/wait; postman updates and notifies; only two communications with the box (place sticker; collect after notify).</p>
<p>Advantage: unnecessary waiting / system time not wasted when threads can communicate.</p>
<h3>12:00 — Core rule (board)</h3>
<pre class='code'>/*
Two threads can communicate with each other
by using wait, notify and notifyAll methods

The thread which is expecting updation
is responsible to call wait method
→ immediately the thread will enter into waiting state

The thread which is responsible to perform updation
after performing updation
is responsible to call notify method
→ then waiting thread will get that notification
and continue its execution with those updated items
*/</pre>
<h3>17:19 — Why `wait`/`notify`/`notifyAll` are in `Object`, not `Thread`</h3>
<p>Fact (SCJP favorite): methods present in `Object` class, not in Thread class.</p>
<p>Wrong student reason: “maybe Thread class didn’t have enough space.” Space is not the issue.</p>
<p>Wrong shallow reason: “so Thread inherits them too” — then everything could live in Object.</p>
<p>Real reason:</p>
<ul><li>You call start() / join() only on Thread objects → those methods belong in Thread</li></ul>
<ul><li>You call wait() / notify() / notifyAll() on any Java object that is being waited on (post-box, Stack, Queue, Student, Customer, …)</li></ul>
<ul><li>If a method must be callable on every Java object → it must live in `Object`</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>/*
wait, notify, notifyAll methods present in Object class
but not in Thread class
because thread can call these methods on any Java object
*/
// Illustrative contrast
Thread t = new Thread();
t.start();   // only on Thread → defined in Thread
t.join();    // only on Thread → defined in Thread

Object postBox = new Object();
// any object can be wait/notify target → defined in Object
synchronized (postBox) {
    postBox.wait();     // expecting updation
    // ...
    postBox.notify();   // after updation
}</pre>
<h3>28:15 — Must be owner / must be in synchronized area</h3>
<p>To call wait on an object, the thread must be owner of that object = must have the lock of that object = must be inside synchronized area for that object.</p>
<p>Analogy: you can wait on your post-box; waiting on neighbor’s post-box → trouble / exception.</p>
<p>If you call wait / notify / notifyAll outside synchronized method/block → runtime exception: IllegalMonitorStateException (monitor ≈ lock / OS semaphore idea).</p>
<pre class='code'>/*
To call wait or notify or notifyAll methods on any object,
thread should be owner of that object
that is the thread should has lock of that object
that is the thread should be inside synchronized area

Hence we can call wait, notify and notifyAll methods
only from synchronized area
otherwise we will get runtime exception saying
IllegalMonitorStateException
*/
class Bad {
    public void m1() {
        try {
            wait(); // not in synchronized area
            // Runtime: IllegalMonitorStateException
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
    }
}

class Good {
    public synchronized void m1() {
        try {
            wait(); // OK — thread owns this object&#x27;s lock
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>Three conclusions so far:</p>
<ol><li>Threads communicate via wait / notify / notifyAll</li></ol>
<ol><li>Those methods live in Object, not Thread</li></ol>
<ol><li>Call only from synchronized area, else IllegalMonitorStateException</li></ol>
<h3>35:26 — Effect of `wait` on the lock (immediately; that object only)</h3>
<p>If T1 calls wait on an object:</p>
<ol><li>It immediately releases the lock of that particular object</li></ol>
<ol><li>Then enters waiting state</li></ol>
<p>Two critical words:</p>
<ul><li>immediately</li></ul>
<ul><li>lock of that particular object only — not all locks the thread may hold</li></ul>
<p>If T1 holds 10 locks and calls wait on one object → releases only that one lock.</p>
<p>Why release? Otherwise the updating thread (postman) cannot acquire the lock to update and notify.</p>
<pre class='code'>/*
If a thread calls wait method on any object
it immediately releases the lock of that particular object
and entered into waiting state
*/</pre>
<h3>39:05 — Effect of `notify` (releases lock, but may NOT immediately)</h3>
<p>Long postman digression (old cycle + bell + uniform vs modern Splendor, post-office policies, packers &amp; movers) → two postman styles:</p>
<ol><li>Deliver / update first, shout “post!” (notify) last</li></ol>
<ol><li>Shout “post!” first, then still search/hand over letter (work after notify)</li></ol>
<p>So both patterns exist: update-then-notify, or notify-then-more-work.</p>
<p>Therefore:</p>
<pre class='code'>/*
If a thread calls notify method on any object
it releases the lock of that object
but may not immediately
*/</pre>
<p>After notify, the notifier may still need to do work while holding the lock; lock release happens later when leaving the synchronized area.</p>
<p>Except wait / notify / notifyAll, there is no other method where a thread releases the lock (for this classical model):</p>
<p>Why must wait/notify release? Waiting thread gave up lock so updater can enter; notifier must eventually release so waiter can re-enter synchronized area and continue.</p>
<h3>51:38 — SCJP-style “which is valid?” checklist</h3>
<p>He frames options:</p>
<ol><li>wait → immediately waiting without releasing any lock → INVALID</li></ol>
<ol><li>wait → releases lock of that object but may not immediately → INVALID (wait is immediate)</li></ol>
<ol><li>wait → releases all locks acquired by that thread and enters waiting → INVALID (only that object’s lock)</li></ol>
<ol><li>wait → immediately releases lock of that particular object and enters waiting → VALID</li></ol>
<ol><li>notify → immediately releases lock of that particular object → INVALID (word “immediately” wrong for notify)</li></ol>
<ol><li>notify → releases lock of that object but may not immediately → VALID</li></ol>
<p>Correct: 4 and 6.</p>
<p>Five theoretical conclusions he asks students to cross-check:</p>
<ol><li>Communicate via wait / notify / notifyAll</li></ol>
<ol><li>Present in Object, not Thread</li></ol>
<ol><li>Only from synchronized area else IllegalMonitorStateException</li></ol>
<ol><li>wait → immediately release that object’s lock → waiting</li></ol>
<ol><li>notify → release that object’s lock but may not immediately; except these three, no other method releases the lock</li></ol>
<h3>58:39 — Method prototypes in `Object`</h3>
<pre class='code'>public final void wait() throws InterruptedException
// wait forever until notification

public final native void wait(long ms) throws InterruptedException
// wait at most ms milliseconds; then continue even if no notify

public final void wait(long ms, int ns) throws InterruptedException
// wait ms + ns (stricter timing)

public final native void notify()

public final native void notifyAll()</pre>
<p>Notes from lecture:</p>
<ul><li>wait is overloaded (three forms)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Of the wait family: the wait(long) form is native; he says first and third are not native in the way he presents (stress: only one wait is native; notify and notifyAll are both native)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Calling wait → release lock → waiting state; another thread may interrupt → `InterruptedException`</li></ul>
<ul><li>Every wait method throws InterruptedException (checked) → must handle with try/catch or throws, else CE</li></ul>
<ul><li>notify / notifyAll do not throw InterruptedException (no waiting state from notify itself)</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>/*
Every wait method throws InterruptedException
which is a checked exception
Hence whenever we are using wait method
compulsory we should handle this InterruptedException
either by try-catch or by throws keyword
otherwise we will get compile time error
*/
class Demo {
    public synchronized void expectUpdate() {
        // CE if InterruptedException not handled:
        // wait();
        try {
            wait();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:06:38 — Impact on thread life-cycle / transition diagram</h3>
<p>Normal path he redraws:</p>
<ol><li>new MyThread() → New / Born</li></ol>
<ol><li>t.start() → Ready / Runnable</li></ol>
<ol><li>Thread scheduler allocates processor → Running</li></ol>
<ol><li>run() completes → Dead</li></ol>
<p>New transition: running thread calls wait() (e.g. obj.wait(), obj.wait(1000), obj.wait(1000, 100)) → Waiting state (and immediately releases that object’s lock).</p>
<p>How to leave waiting (any of):</p>
<ul><li>got notification</li></ul>
<ul><li>time expires (timed wait)</li></ul>
<ul><li>got interrupted</li></ul>
<p>Exam trap: after leaving wait, does the thread go directly to Running? Or Ready/Runnable?</p>
<p>Students often say Ready/Runnable. He says that transition is wrong for the full story.</p>
<p>After wait, to continue you need two things: notification (or timeout/interrupt path) and the lock again. Notification alone is not enough if the notifier still holds the lock (notify may not release immediately).</p>
<p>So: waiting thread got notification → enters another waiting state — to get lock.</p>
<p>When waiting thread got the lock → then Ready/Runnable → scheduler → Running → eventually Dead.</p>
<pre class='code'>/*
Life cycle (wait path):

New/Born --t.start()--&gt; Ready/Runnable
Ready/Runnable --scheduler allocates CPU--&gt; Running
Running --run completes--&gt; Dead

Running --obj.wait() / wait(ms) / wait(ms,ns)--&gt; Waiting
Waiting --notification OR time expires OR interrupted--&gt;
         Another waiting state (to get lock)
Another waiting (for lock) --got lock--&gt; Ready/Runnable
Ready/Runnable --scheduler--&gt; Running
*/

/*
VERY IMPORTANT (SCJP):
If waiting thread got notification,
it will NOT go to Running directly
and NOT simply &quot;Ready&quot; as the only next box in a naive diagram —
it goes to another waiting state to get the lock
(because after notify, lock may not be released immediately)
*/</pre>
<h3>1:18:09 — Close of Part-1</h3>
<p>Theoretical foundations of inter-thread communication, Object vs Thread placement, synchronized requirement, wait vs notify lock-release timing, method signatures, and the waiting→waiting-for-lock life-cycle twist. Practical programs continue in later parts.</p>
